<h1 id="Introduction">Introduction<a class="anchor-link" href="#Introduction">¶</a></h1><p>In this assignment, our primary objective is to enhance the predictive capabilities of a machine learning model by performing feature engineering on the provided dataset. The focus will be on addressing missing values in continuous variables and implementing mean encoding for categorical variables. Subsequently, we will train a decision tree model using these newly engineered features and evaluate its performance.</p>
<p>Feature engineering is a crucial step in the data preprocessing pipeline, as it helps transform raw data into a format that is more suitable for machine learning algorithms. The quality and relevance of features directly impact model accuracy, interpretability, and generalization ability. In real-world datasets, missing data is a prevalent issue that can significantly influence the model’s predictive power if not handled properly. Therefore, one of our key tasks is to address missing values in continuous variables to prevent biases and inconsistencies in the dataset.</p>
<p>Additionally, categorical variables need to be encoded appropriately to ensure they are effectively utilized by the model. In this assignment, we will apply Mean Encoding, a technique that replaces each categorical variable with the mean of the target variable for each category. Unlike one-hot encoding or label encoding, mean encoding captures the relationship between categorical features and the target variable, potentially leading to better model performance. However, to prevent overfitting, we must ensure that this encoding method is applied carefully, possibly using techniques like smoothing or k-fold target encoding.</p>
<p>Once the dataset has been preprocessed and feature engineering techniques have been applied, we will train a Decision Tree Model using these enhanced features. Decision trees are widely used in predictive modeling due to their interpretability and ability to capture complex relationships in data. We will assess the performance of this model and compare it with the previous model built in Assignment 3, which used raw or less refined features. The objective is to determine whether the improvements in feature engineering result in better model accuracy, stability, and generalization to unseen data.</p>
<p>By following this systematic approach, we aim to emphasize the critical role of feature engineering in machine learning. The results from this study will provide insights into how well-processed features contribute to predictive model performance and demonstrate the significance of data preprocessing in real-world applications. Through this assignment, we seek to enhance our understanding of feature engineering techniques and develop practical skills that are essential in building robust and efficient machine learning models.</p>
