<h1 id="Important-Consideration:">Important Consideration:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Important-Consideration:">¶</a></h1><p>In this assignment, our main objective is to build a model that can be compared to the model from the previous assignment in a meaningful way. For this, we need to ensure that both models are based on the same set of features. This will help us make an accurate comparison of the performance and effectiveness of both models.</p>
<h3 id="Procedure-and-Justifications:">Procedure and Justifications:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Procedure-and-Justifications:">¶</a></h3><p><strong>1. Loading and Identifying Features:</strong></p>
<p>First, we will load the dataset and distinguish between numerical and categorical features. This step is important because the type of feature (numerical or categorical) will determine how we handle the missing values and apply transformations. By identifying these features clearly, we can ensure that we treat them appropriately in our model.</p>
<p><strong>2. Consistency in Columns:</strong></p>
<p>The columns used in the new model should be the same as those used in the previous model. This is critical because the comparison of model performance can only be accurate if both models are working with the same data. If the feature columns differ, the results will be skewed, making the comparison invalid. Thus, after splitting the dataset into training and testing sets, we will verify that both sets contain the same feature columns.</p>
<p><strong>3. Filling Missing Values in the Previous Model:</strong></p>
<p>In the previous model, we handled missing values by filling them with:</p>
<ul>
<li>Median for numerical features: The median is often a better choice for numerical data because it is less affected by extreme values (outliers) compared to the mean.</li>
<li>Mode for categorical features: The mode represents the most frequent category, and filling missing values with the mode helps maintain the distribution of categories.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>4. Filling Missing Values in the New Model:</strong></p>
<p>In the new model, we are using different imputation strategies:</p>
<ul>
<li>For continuous variables, we will fill missing values with the mean. This approach is simpler and can work well when the data distribution is relatively balanced and not significantly skewed.</li>
<li>For categorical variables, we will apply mean encoding, which involves encoding categorical features by replacing them with the mean of the target variable for each category. This method can be more informative compared to using the mode, as it captures the relationship between the categorical feature and the target variable, potentially improving model performance.</li>
</ul>
<p>By following these steps, we ensure that the dataset is treated in a consistent and structured way, making it easier to compare the models and understand how different feature handling strategies impact performance. This approach will allow us to test and refine the model more effectively.</p>
