<p><em>The output we are getting above, with no discrepancies between the columns in the training and test sets, indicates that both the training and test sets contain the exact same features. This is a good thing because it ensures that no features were dropped or missed during the train-test split.</em></p>
<p>Here’s a breakdown of what this output means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Columns in training but not in test: set():</strong> This means that there are no columns in the training data that are not present in the test data. In other words, all the columns used for training are also available in the test set.</li>
<li><strong>Columns in test but not in training: set():</strong> This means that there are no columns in the test data that are not present in the training data. Thus, all the columns used in the test set are also available in the training data.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="Why-This-Is-Important:">Why This Is Important:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Why-This-Is-Important:">¶</a></h4><ul>
<li><p><strong>Consistency:</strong> It confirms that the training and test sets are aligned in terms of features. The model will see the same features in both the training and testing phases, ensuring fair evaluation and avoiding errors related to missing features during testing.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>No Data Leakage:</strong> Since the features are consistent across the training and test datasets, there’s no risk of data leakage. This ensures that the model is not biased by any unwanted knowledge about the test set during the training phase.</p>
</li>
</ul>
