<h2 id="3.2-Key-Findings-and-Observations">3.2 Key Findings and Observations<a class="anchor-link" href="#3.2-Key-Findings-and-Observations">¶</a></h2><p>In our latest experiment, we observed a slight drop in accuracy from 85.38% in the previous model to 85.12% in the current model. While the change is minimal, several factors may have contributed to this performance shift, particularly the differences in handling missing values and encoding categorical variables. Below is a breakdown of the key observations and potential improvements:</p>
<h3 id="1.-Handling-Missing-Values:-Median-vs.-Mean-Imputation">1. Handling Missing Values: Median vs. Mean Imputation<a class="anchor-link" href="#1.-Handling-Missing-Values:-Median-vs.-Mean-Imputation">¶</a></h3><p><strong>Previous Model (Median Imputation):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pros:</strong> Median imputation is robust to outliers, making it a suitable choice for datasets with skewed distributions. It ensures that extreme values do not unduly influence the imputation process, resulting in stable and representative feature values.</li>
<li><strong>Impact on Accuracy:</strong> The more stable feature distributions likely contributed to the consistent performance of the previous model.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Current Model (Mean Imputation):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cons:</strong> The mean is sensitive to outliers, and if the dataset contains extreme values, these can distort the imputed values, leading to biased feature distributions.</li>
<li><strong>Impact on Accuracy:</strong> This could explain the slight accuracy drop, as the model may have learned from slightly skewed data, leading to less optimal predictions.</li>
<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Given the dataset's characteristics, median imputation may be a better fit, especially if outliers are present in the continuous variables.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="2.-Encoding-Categorical-Variables:-Mode-Imputation-vs.-Mean-Encoding">2. Encoding Categorical Variables: Mode Imputation vs. Mean Encoding<a class="anchor-link" href="#2.-Encoding-Categorical-Variables:-Mode-Imputation-vs.-Mean-Encoding">¶</a></h3><p><strong>Previous Model (Mode Imputation + Label Encoding/One-Hot Encoding):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pros:</strong> Mode imputation preserves the original categorical distribution, and encoding methods like Label Encoding or One-Hot Encoding treat each category independently, making them robust for capturing categorical relationships.</li>
<li><strong>Impact on Accuracy:</strong> The consistency of encoding likely ensured that categorical features were well-represented without introducing data leakage or overfitting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Current Model (Mean Encoding):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Cons:</strong> Mean encoding replaces categories with the mean of the target variable, which can introduce data leakage if not handled carefully. This method might also lead to overfitting, particularly when the distributions of categories differ between the training and test sets.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Impact on Accuracy:</strong> The minor drop in accuracy could be attributed to this encoding approach, which may have led to overfitting or incorrect representation of categorical features, impacting the model's generalization.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> To mitigate the risk of overfitting, techniques like Target Smoothing or K-Fold Mean Encoding should be explored. In some cases, One-Hot Encoding may outperform mean encoding, especially if categorical variables have fewer unique categories.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="3.-Sensitivity-of-Decision-Trees-to-Data-Preprocessing">3. Sensitivity of Decision Trees to Data Preprocessing<a class="anchor-link" href="#3.-Sensitivity-of-Decision-Trees-to-Data-Preprocessing">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li><strong>Impact on Splitting:</strong> Decision trees create splits based on feature values, and any modification in the distribution of continuous or categorical features can alter the way splits are made, which may slightly affect the model's decision boundaries.</li>
<li><strong>Changes in the Data:</strong> Both mean imputation and mean encoding modify feature distributions, which in turn can influence the splits and potentially lead to minor shifts in performance.</li>
<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> The slight accuracy drop is consistent with the sensitivity of decision trees to feature distributions. Careful consideration of feature engineering strategies is essential to maintain model performance.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="Final-Thoughts:">Final Thoughts:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Final-Thoughts:">¶</a></h3><p>The small decrease in accuracy from <strong>85.38% to 85.12%</strong> suggests the following conclusions:</p>
<ul>
<li>Median Imputation is likely more appropriate for handling continuous variables, particularly in the presence of outliers, and could help restore accuracy.</li>
<li>Mean Encoding should be applied with caution, as it can lead to overfitting or data leakage. Exploring techniques like Target Smoothing or K-Fold Mean Encoding could improve results.</li>
<li>A hybrid strategy, combining Median Imputation with One-Hot Encoding for categorical variables, may yield better performance without the risks associated with mean encoding.</li>
</ul>
