<h2 id="Performance-Metrics-Comparison-&amp;-Analysis">Performance Metrics Comparison &amp; Analysis<a class="anchor-link" href="#Performance-Metrics-Comparison-&amp;-Analysis">¶</a></h2><p>We observed a slight decrease in performance across key classification metrics after applying new feature engineering techniques. Below is a detailed comparison:</p>
<ul>
<li>Metric	  -  Previous Model  -  Current Model -   Change</li>
<li>Precision  -	0.1448      -	0.1368  -	⬇ Decreased</li>
<li>Recall	  -  0.1671   -  	0.1604	-   ⬇ Decreased</li>
<li>F1 Score   - 	0.1552  -	0.1477  -	⬇ Decreased</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="Key-Observations:">Key Observations:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Key-Observations:">¶</a></h3><p><strong>1. Precision Drop (0.1448 → 0.1368)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Precision measures the proportion of correctly predicted positive cases out of all predicted positives.</li>
<li>The slight drop suggests the model is making more false positives, possibly due to changes in categorical encoding (Mean Encoding) affecting class separability.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Recall Drop (0.1671 → 0.1604)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Recall indicates the proportion of actual positives that were correctly identified.</li>
<li>A lower recall suggests the model is missing more actual positive cases, potentially due to altered decision boundaries caused by mean imputation.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. F1 Score Drop (0.1552 → 0.1477)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The F1 Score, a balance between precision and recall, has slightly decreased, reinforcing the notion that the changes in feature engineering negatively impacted both metrics.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="Possible-Causes-&amp;-Recommendations:">Possible Causes &amp; Recommendations:<a class="anchor-link" href="#Possible-Causes-&amp;-Recommendations:">¶</a></h3><p><strong>1. Mean Encoding Effect:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mean encoding can introduce data leakage and overfitting, leading to reduced precision.</li>
<li>Solution: We can One-Hot Encoding or K-Fold Target Encoding to improve generalization.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Mean Imputation for Missing Values:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mean imputation may have skewed feature distributions, affecting recall.</li>
<li>Solution: We can use Median Imputation, especially for features with outliers.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Decision Tree Sensitivity:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Decision Trees are highly sensitive to how data is preprocessed, meaning even small changes in feature values can affect splits.</li>
<li>Solution: By considering Hyperparameter Tuning (e.g., max depth, min samples split) to make the tree more robust.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="Final-Thoughts">Final Thoughts<a class="anchor-link" href="#Final-Thoughts">¶</a></h3><p>While the performance drop is minor, it suggests that the changes in feature engineering have slightly reduced model effectiveness. We recommend experimenting with different encoding and imputation strategies to determine the best approach.</p>
