<p>This assignment highlights the critical role of feature engineering in model performance, demonstrating how different preprocessing techniques can impact accuracy. By comparing Median Imputation &amp; Mode Encoding with Mean Imputation &amp; Mean Encoding, we observed a slight accuracy drop from 85.38% to 85.12%, indicating that mean imputation might have skewed feature distributions, particularly in the presence of outliers. Additionally, mean encoding, while useful, introduced potential overfitting risks by incorporating target information directly into feature values. Decision Trees, being highly sensitive to data transformations, were affected by these changes, emphasizing the importance of selecting appropriate imputation and encoding techniques. Moving forward, a hybrid strategy combining Median Imputation for continuous variables and One-Hot Encoding or K-Fold Mean Encoding for categorical features could enhance model generalization. Furthermore, exploring more robust models like Random Forest or XGBoost, alongside hyperparameter tuning and cross-validation, would ensure better performance and stability. Ultimately, this analysis underscores the need for carefully tailored feature engineering strategies, as even small modifications in preprocessing can significantly impact predictive outcomes.</p>
